# Copy to .dev.vars and fill in. Documents required env for local dev.

# Base URL of the remote backend (auth + the systems registry). Must include
# the scheme — http:// or https://. Optional: leave it out and the dashboard
# runs with no backend (screens render, data calls fail with one clear error).
NEST_API_URL="https://staging-backend.ramaaz.store"

# Nothing else is needed. Sign-in carries no build-time credential: the access
# link's token arrives in the URL at /enter/<token> and is exchanged for a
# challenge — see src/lib/auth/link.ts.

# Which face check runs at the sign-in's face step. Unset (the default) = AWS
# Rekognition Face Liveness, which is what ships. `single-frame` falls back to
# one captured frame + CompareFaces — WEAKER: a photo of the admin on a second
# phone passes it. It exists for an AWS outage, and flipping it needs BOTH
# sides: the KYC Worker must accept `liveFaceImageData` for the root tenant
# again, or the capture ends in a generic failure. Any other value = liveness.
KYC_FACE_MODE=""

# Which AWS region Face Liveness sessions stream to. Read ONLY by the CSP in
# src/middleware.ts, which must name that host: the browser streams the selfie
# video straight to AWS, so `connect-src 'self'` would otherwise block it.
#
# Unset = us-east-1, which is what ships.
#
# MUST MATCH the region the KYC Worker mints sessions in. The Worker calls
# CreateFaceLivenessSession and hands the browser back a `region`; this only
# decides which host the policy permits. Set them differently and the socket is
# refused with nothing in the network tab to explain it.
#
# Worth moving: distance to this region is the dominant cost of the whole face
# check. Measured from a phone in the Middle East against us-east-1, the wait
# between the camera going dark and AWS answering was 21-28s, of which the
# inference is a few. Check the current Face Liveness supported-region list
# before picking — it is narrower than Rekognition's overall.
KYC_LIVENESS_REGION=""

# Proves to the backend that the X-Edge-Client-IP / -Country headers came from
# this Worker and may be trusted. See src/lib/api/edge.ts.
#
# ⚠️ DELIBERATELY ABSENT LOCALLY, and absent from this file on purpose — do not
# add it to .dev.vars. The backend's rule for local development is to send no
# edge headers at all rather than empty ones, and that falls out of the secret
# being unset: no secret, no headers, and your machine is treated as the caller
# with its own rate-limit budget. Development links are issued without address
# or country conditions, so nothing needs a country.
#
# In a deployed environment set it as a Cloudflare SECRET
# (`npx wrangler secret put EDGE_SECRET`), one value PER ENVIRONMENT — staging
# and production never share one — and never as a wrangler.jsonc `vars` entry,
# which would shadow the secret and commit the value.
#
# Rotation needs no lockstep deploy: the backend accepts the old and new values
# while a rotation is in progress.

# Unlocks /design in a DEPLOYED environment. Leave unset locally — the gallery
# is always on in development. In production set it as a Cloudflare SECRET
# (`npx wrangler secret put DESIGN_GALLERY_KEY`), never as a wrangler.jsonc
# `vars` entry: a vars entry shadows a secret of the same name, and would commit
# the value. Unlock a device once at /design/unlock?key=<value>.
DESIGN_GALLERY_KEY=""

# Unlocks the liveness bench at /design/liveness-lab — runs the real AWS Face
# Liveness check with no sign-in around it, so a spoof can be attempted over and
# over. Must match LIVENESS_LAB_SECRET on the ramaaz-kyc worker. Unset on both
# sides = the routes 404 and the bench is gone. Each run costs one check.
LIVENESS_LAB_SECRET=""
